  "other_facts": "Fast transport status, acquired with time on peritoneal dialysis (PD), is a pathology induced by peritoneal exposure to bioincompatible solutions. Fast transport has important clinical consequences and should be prevented. Membrane prognosis and technique survival were independent of baseline transport characteristics. Fast transport and ultrafiltration (UF) failure are reversible conditions, provided that peritonitis and high glucose exposure are avoided during the early dialysis period. The first year on PD is a main determining factor for the membrane's future. The mass transfer coefficient of creatinine at year 1 is the best functional predictor of future PD history. After 5 years on dialysis, permeability frequently increases, and UF decreases. Icodextrin is associated with peritoneal protection. Peritoneal kinetic studies were performed at baseline and annually using a 4-hour dwell with glucose (1.5% during 1981-1990, and 2.27% during 1991-2002) to calculate the peritoneal mass transfer coefficients of urea and creatinine (milliliters per minute) using a previously described mathematical model.",
  "summary": "This cohort study of 275 peritoneal dialysis patients found that membrane prognosis is independent of baseline transport characteristics, but fast transport and UF failure are reversible if peritonitis and high glucose exposure are avoided early, and that icodextrin is associated with peritoneal protection."
}

