  "other_facts": "Normal human fetal development requires adequate thyroid hormone from conception. Until about 16 wk gestation, supply is entirely by placental transfer of maternal hormone. Subsequently, fetal thyroid synthesizes hormones requiring maternal iodide. Trophoblast iodide transfer mediated by apical sodium iodide symporter (NIS). Placental oxygen levels low in early pregnancy (~1%), rising with placental vascularisation to a plateau of ~8% at about 16 wk. Desferrioxamine (iron chelator and hypoxia mimic) decreased NIS and hCG expression and I-125 uptake in BeWo cells. NIS expression and I-125 uptake in cells grown at 1% oxygen were not increased by addition of hCG (2,500 IU/l). Inference: placental NIS mRNA and protein expression are regulated by oxygen, rising with vascularization of the placenta in the late first trimester, a time when fetal iodide requirements are increasing.",
