  "other_facts": "537 men had longitudinal DXA data with 4.5 years of follow-up. 432 men experienced incident nonvertebral fractures, including 81 hip fractures. Higher 25OHD was associated with higher baseline BMD, slower BMD loss, and lower hip fracture risk. Men with higher 1,25(OH)2 D had lower baseline BMD. 1,25(OH)2 D was not associated with BMD loss or nonvertebral fracture. Lowest 1,25(OH)2 D levels range: 8.70 to 51.60 pg/mL. Associations of 25OHD with skeletal outcomes were generally stronger than those for 1,25(OH)2 D. Interactions between 25OHD and 1,25(OH)2 D were tested for each outcome. Adjustment of 1,25(OH)2 D data for 25OHD (and vice versa) had little effect on the associations observed but did attenuate the hip fracture association of both vitamin D metabolites. These results do not support the hypothesis that measures of 1,25(OH)2 D improve the ability to predict adverse skeletal outcomes when 25OHD measures are available.",
  "summary": "In older men, higher 1,25(OH)2 D was associated with lower baseline BMD but not with bone loss or nonvertebral fracture risk, while after BMD adjustment a protective association for hip fracture was found; 25OHD associations were generally stronger."
